Asteroid Collision:
We are given an array asteroids of integers representing asteroids in a row. The indices of the asteroid in the array represent their relative position in space.
For each asteroid, the absolute value represents its size, and the sign represents its direction (positive meaning right, negative meaning left). Each asteroid moves at the same speed.
Find out the state of the asteroids after all collisions. If two asteroids meet, the smaller one will explode. If both are the same size, both will explode. Two asteroids moving in the same direction will never meet.
 

Solution Approach:
By using Monotonic Logic, we can achieve this one.





Code:
public int[] asteroidCollision(int[] arr) {
int n = arr.length;
Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
for(int i=0;i<n;i++) {
	if(!stack.isEmpty() && arr[i] < 0 && stack.peek() > 0) {
		int temp = arr[i] * -1;
		while(!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek() < temp && stack.peek() > 0) {
			stack.pop();
		}
		if(!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek() == temp) {
			stack.pop();
			continue;
		}
		if(!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek() > temp) {
			continue;
		}
	}			
	stack.push(arr[i]);			
}

int len = stack.size();
int[] res = new int[len];
for(int i = len - 1; i >= 0; i--){
    res[i] = stack.pop();
}
return res;
}






Implementing Queue Using Stack:
Implement a first in first out (FIFO) queue using only two stacks. The implemented queue should support all the functions of a normal queue (push, peek, pop, and empty).
Implement the MyQueue class:
	void push(int x) Pushes element x to the back of the queue.
	int pop() Removes the element from the front of the queue and returns it.
	int peek() Returns the element at the front of the queue.
	boolean empty() Returns true if the queue is empty, false otherwise.
Notes:
You must use only standard operations of a stack, which means only push to top, peek/pop from top, size, and is empty operations are valid.

Solution Approach:
	For the question itself they mentioned 2 stacks, so will use 2 stacks. One for Input and another one for output.
	The given 4 methods, push() and empty() we can directly use the stack operations.
	But for the pop() and peek() methods only decide whether queue or stack. So for those 2 methods we need to write logic.
	The logic is whenever the 2 methods are calling that time we have to update output with condition. The condition is output stack have to update when its empty.
	By this condition, output stack wont update for every pop() and peek() call.
	What if we push another value? It will be on our input stack, so when our output stack is empty will update it using input stack. Its kind of refilling. In FIFO, ex. First 3(in – 3,2,1) values are in outpu	t stack (out – 1,2,3) and its all are pop() then, then will fill the it with newly added value(in – 5,4) => out – 4,5. So when do pop()/peek() it will return 4.
Complexity
Operation	Complexity
Push()	O(1)
Pop()	O(1) amortized
Peek()	O(1) amortized
Empty()	O(1)
You might wonder why pop() is O(1) amortized even though it sometimes contains a loop.
That's because each element is transferred from input to output only once.



Code:
class MyQueue {
    Stack<Integer> input;
    Stack<Integer> output;
    public MyQueue() {
        input = new Stack<>();
        output = new Stack<>();        
    }   
    public void push(int x) {        
        input.push(x);
    }    
    public int pop() {
        moveToOutput();
        return output.pop();
    }    
    public int peek() {
        moveToOutput();
        return output.peek();
    }    
    public boolean empty() {
        return input.isEmpty() && output.isEmpty();
    }
    public void moveToOutput(){
        if(output.isEmpty()){
            while(!input.isEmpty()){
                output.push(input.pop());
            }
        }
    }
}
Backspace String Compare
Given two strings s and t, return true if they are equal when both are typed into empty text editors. '#' means a backspace character.
 
Solution Approach:
	Will use 2 stacks and in stacks store the characters without # with character (ex. b#)
	Then compare the 2 stacks and return true if all characters are same in both stacks, otherwise false.
Code:
public boolean backspaceCompare(String s, String t) {
        Stack<Character> stack_s = RemoveBackSpace(s);
        Stack<Character> stack_t = RemoveBackSpace(t);
        
        if(stack_s.size() != stack_t.size()){
            return false;
        }
        while(!stack_s.isEmpty() && !stack_t.isEmpty()){
            if(stack_s.pop() != stack_t.pop()){
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
    private static Stack<Character> RemoveBackSpace(String s){
        Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<>();
        for(char ch: s.toCharArray()){
            if(ch!='#'){
                stack.push(ch);
            }else if(!stack.isEmpty()) {
                stack.pop();
            }
        }
        return stack;
    }




