Merge Two Sorted List:
Merge the two sorted lists into one sorted list. Return the head of the merged linked list.
 

Solution Approach:
•	At linked list, all the given input of linked list are pointer of head node. Here also they given the header node of 2 lists.
•	As they want header node of merged list, first we will create res object of listNode. Then create another pointer which will point the res pointer. 
•	Iterate the list and compare the values, if any value Is small then assign that node address to our res’s next. 
•	Will move our res to the res.next pointer to frame the list.
•	Lastly will return the header node’s next which points the res at starting point. 
•	Why header.next? while creating res pointer, we will create as -1 value and null as next. After that only will be started assigning the actual sorted list. So returning header will make the list as -1, 1, 1, 2..  So to avoid the -1 at initial, we are returning header.next.

Code:
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * public class ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode next;
 *     ListNode() {}
 *     ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode list1, ListNode list2) {
        ListNode res = new ListNode(-1);
        ListNode head = res;
        while(list1 != null && list2 != null){
            if(list1.val < list2.val){
                res.next = list1;
                list1 = list1.next;
            }else{
                res.next = list2;
                list2 = list2.next;
            }
            res = res.next;
        }
        res.next = (list1 !=null)? list1 : list2; //Here we are assigning the remaining listNode which is not null
        return head.next;       
    }
}

Reverse Linked List:
Given the head of a singly linked list, reverse the list, and return the reversed list.
Ex. Input:  1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5
Output: 5 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1

Solution Approach:
•	After reverse the list, it should be like, 1 <- 2 <- 3 <- 4 <- 5 and we will return the 5th node.
•	Here we will use 2 pointers which is prev and next. There is head pointer(which will come as input).
•	So change the direction from (left to right) to (right to left) will use the prev and next pointer.

•	Consider, prev – head – next. 
o	Before change the head.next value, will assign it to the next node
o	Once assigned, will change the head.next as prev.
o	Once prev address is saved, then change the prev to header.
o	Then header pointer will change to next.
o	By doing this, we are changing each nodes pointer
•	At the end will next will get null and changing header as next then header will also null.
•	So will return the prev.

Code:
public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {
        ListNode prev = null;
        while(head != null){
            ListNode next = head.next;
            head.next = prev;
            prev = head;
            head = next;
        }
        return prev;
}


Middle of Linked List:
Given the head of a singly linked list, return the middle node of the linked list.
If there are two middle nodes, return the second middle node.
 
Solution Approach:
•	Slow and Fast Pointer Approach:
o	Slow pointer will move by 1x
o	Fast pointer will move by 2x
o	Once Fast pointer is null or Fast pointer.next is null then return slow pointer.
	(Fast == null) => Even case
	(Fast.next == null) => Odd case.

Code:
public ListNode middleNode(ListNode head) {
        ListNode slow = head;
        ListNode fast = head;
        while(fast != null && fast.next != null){
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }
        return slow;
    }

Remove Nth Node From End of List:
Given the head of a linked list, remove the nth node from the end of the list and return its head.
Ex. head = [1,2,3,4,5], n = 2
Output: [1,2,3,5]

Solution Approach:
Brute-Force: 
	run a loop for n times to find the size.
	Once found the length of list, then len – k to find node have to be remove.
	Then will change the pointer.
	It will take O(2N).
Optimized way:
	Instead of Find the size of the list, move a pointer(fast) for k times.
	Then move a slow pointer (which initially pointing outside of list) and fast pointer simultaneously until fast became null.
	Once fast pointer points null then change slow pointer’s next.
	Difference btw Brute-Force and Optimized way:
o	At brute-force, first loop runs for n times, then n-k times.
o	TC for brute-force will be O(n)+O(n-k).
o	At optimized way, first loop will run for k times, then 2nd loop will run for n-k times, which is remaining nodes from k pointer. 
Code:
public ListNode removeNthFromEnd(ListNode head, int n) {
        ListNode temp =new ListNode(-1, head);
        ListNode slow = temp, fast = temp;
        for(int i=0;i<=n;i++) fast = fast.next;
        while(fast != null){
            fast = fast.next;
            slow = slow.next;
        }
        slow.next = slow.next.next;
        return temp.next; //why returning temp.next? temp initially pointing the head and the slow/fast are pointing to temp only. Slow pointer update the list at end, and slow pointer initial is temp, so temp.next points the head.
    }






