Sort List:
Given the head of a linked list, return the list after sorting it in ascending order.
Example:
Input: head = [4,2,1,3]
Output: [1,2,3,4]
Solution Approach:
	By using merge sort, will sort the list.




Code:
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * public class ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode next;
 *     ListNode() {}
 *     ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public ListNode sortList(ListNode head) {
        if(head==null || head.next ==null){
            return head;
        }
        ListNode middle = middleNode(head);
        ListNode middleNext = middle.next; //As its Linked List, we are separating list from middle.
        middle.next = null; //By setting this null, the linked list no longer linked.

        ListNode leftNode = sortList(head);
        ListNode rightNode = sortList(middleNext);

        return mergeLinkedList(leftNode,rightNode);        
    }

    private ListNode middleNode(ListNode head){
        ListNode slow = head, fast = head, prev = head;
        while(fast!=null && fast.next!=null){
            prev = slow;
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }

        return prev;
    }

    private ListNode mergeLinkedList(ListNode l1, ListNode l2){
        ListNode temp = new ListNode(-1);
        ListNode head = temp;

        while(l1 !=null && l2 !=null){
            if(l1.val < l2.val){
                temp.next = l1;
                l1 = l1.next;
            }else{
                temp.next = l2;
                l2 = l2.next;
            }
            temp = temp.next;
        }

        temp.next = (l1!=null)? l1:l2;

        return head.next;
    }
}








Partition List:
Given the head of a linked list and a value x, partition it such that all nodes less than x come before nodes greater than or equal to x.
You should preserve the original relative order of the nodes in each of the two partitions
 

Solution Approach:
	The Question is, have to arrange lower value of x on left side and arrange higher value of x on right side.
	Brute-force: Traversal the list twice, for the 1st traversal arrange the lower values, at 2nd traversal arrange the greater values.
	Optimized way: Instead of traversal twice, we can arrange low/higher in single traversal.
o	Have to create 2 lists, one for lower values; another one higher values.
o	After that connect the lower list to higher list value.
o	Before returning, Make it null on the end of greater list. 
Code:
public ListNode partition(ListNode head, int x) {
        ListNode less = new ListNode(-1);
        ListNode greater = new ListNode(-1);
        ListNode lhead = less, ghead = greater;
        while(head != null){
            if(head.val < x){
                lhead.next = head;
                head = head.next;
                lhead = lhead.next; 
            }else{
                ghead.next = head;
                head = head.next;
                ghead = ghead.next; 
            }
        }
        ghead.next = null;
        lhead.next = greater.next;
        return less.next;
    }

Palindrome Linked List:
Given the head of a singly linked list, return true if it is a palindrome or false otherwise.
Ex. Head = [1,2,3,3,2,1];
Output: true
Solution Approach:
	We can check palindrome by below methods,
1.	Create a new Reverse of Linked List and Checks.
  1.a.	Extra space – Creating New Linked List
  1.b.	Two times traversal
      1.b.i)	One for reverse
      1.b.ii)	Another one for compare
2.	Create stack and push elements, until reach the middle list; pop elements from stack and check the remaining elements (after middle) in linked list.
  2.a	Extra Space – Stack
3.	Same as above approach(2nd), but without creating stack.
  3.a	Instead of creating a stack, we are reversing the half of the linked (after middle node) and starts compares the 1st and 2nd.

Code:
public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head){
	ListNode firstHalf = head;
	ListNode secondHalf = null; 
	ListNode slow = head;
	ListNode fast = head;
	ListNode middle = slow;
	
	while(fast != null && fast.next != null){
		middle = slow;
		slow = slow.next;
		fast = fast.next.next;
	}	
	ListNode rvsPnt = middle.next; // it traversal list to reverse the list.
	while(rvsPnt !=null){
		ListNode next = rvsPnt.next;
		rvsPnt.next = secondHalf;
		secondHalf = rvsPnt; //once rvsPnt became null, will get the reversed list head on secondHalf
		rvsPnt = next;
	}	
	while(secondHalf!=null){ //secondHalf only we set null
		if(firstHalf.val != secondHalf.val) return false;
		firstHalf = firstHalf.next;
		secondHalf = secondHalf.next;
	}
	
	return true;	
}






